window.OPENBAND_HISTORY={"394": "\u003csection class=\"history-card\" aria-label=\"Brief History\">\u003cdiv class=\"history-heading\">\u003ch3>\u003csvg aria-hidden=\"true\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\u003cpath d=\"M12 5v15M12 5C8 2 4 3 2 4v15c3-1 7-1 10 1 3-2 7-2 10-1V4c-2-1-6-2-10 1Z\"/>\u003c/svg>Brief History\u003c/h3>\u003cspan class=\"history-verified\" title=\"Cited sources checked 2026-11-03\">Sources Verified\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"history-overview\">Canoe Lake Cree First Nation is a Cree community in northwestern Saskatchewan whose land-based history predates Treaty 10. Hunting, fishing and trapping sustained community members across a broad traditional territory before and after treaty signing. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a> On September 19, 1906, Chief John Iron and Headmen Baptiste Iron and Jerome Couilloneur signed Treaty 10 at Île-à-la-Crosse, according to the commissioner’s report, with the Crown’s commissioner, James Andrew Joseph McKenna. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> The creation of the Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range in 1954 restricted access to approximately 75% of the Nation’s most productive traditional lands. A subsequent claim was settled in June 1997. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a> These documented events describe part of the Nation’s treaty-era and later history; treaty records are not a complete account of its older Indigenous history or continuing Cree identity.\u003c/p>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Treaty No. 10 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing date\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>September 19, 1906 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Signing location\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Île-à-la-Crosse, Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Chief\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>John Iron \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cdetails class=\"history-more\">\u003csummary>Read More \u003cspan>Leaders, territory &amp; historical events\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cdl class=\"history-facts history-facts-secondary\">\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical Headmen\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Baptiste Iron, Jerome Couilloneur \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Treaty Commissioner\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>James Andrew Joseph McKenna \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/about/historical-biographies/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Historical Biographies\">[4]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Traditional language\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Cree (Nêhiyaw) \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Historical territory\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>Northwestern Saskatchewan \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Our History\">[2]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv>\u003cdt>Land claim settlement\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>June 1997 \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>1954 · Primrose Lake Air Weapons Range\u003c/strong> — The range’s establishment removed access to about three quarters of the Nation’s most productive traditional lands, disrupting land-based livelihoods. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cstrong>1975–1997 · Claim and settlement\u003c/strong> — Canoe Lake submitted a claim in 1975 for the loss of traditional territory. Canada accepted it in 1995, and a settlement followed in June 1997. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Canoe Lake First Nation\">[3]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">Signing location: McKenna’s contemporary report places the Canoe Lake band’s September 19 signing at Île-à-la-Crosse. A later OTC account lists Canoe Lake. The location above follows the contemporary report; the date is consistent across these accounts. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/otc-at-the-treaty-10-gathering/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: OTC at the Treaty 10 Gathering\">[8]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdiv class=\"history-connections\">\u003ch4>Historical Connections\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ch5>\u003ca href=\"/first-nations/english-river-dene-nation/\">English River First Nation\u003c/a> \u003cspan>Present-day First Nation\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chief William Apisis signed Treaty 10 at Île-à-la-Crosse on August 28, 1906. The English River and Canoe Lake bands participated in the same 1906 treaty process. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\">[1]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://otc.ca/about/historical-biographies/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Historical Biographies\">[4]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ch5>Clear Lake Band \u003cspan>Historical band\u003c/span>\u003c/h5>\u003cp>Chief Raphael Bedshi-Dekkge signed Treaty 10 in 1906 for the historical Clear Lake Band, another participant in the original negotiations. Later known as the Peter Pond Band, it divided in 1972 into Buffalo River and Turnor Lake, now Birch Narrows Dene Nation. \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/fra/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Treaty No. 10 · French transcription\">[7]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Birch Narrows Dene Nation Community Plan\">[5]\u003c/a> \u003ca class=\"history-citation\" href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Source: Buffalo River Dene First Nation\">[9]\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"history-related\">Present-day profiles: \u003ca href=\"/first-nations/birch-narrows-dene-nation/\">Birch Narrows Dene Nation\u003c/a> · \u003ca href=\"/first-nations/buffalo-river-dene-nation/\">Buffalo River Dene Nation\u003c/a>\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">These are shared historical treaty relationships. The historical Clear Lake Band is distinct from today’s Nations; these links do not imply common language, ancestry, governance or a political alliance.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"history-sources\">\u003csummary>View Sources \u003cspan>8 cited sources\u003c/span>\u003c/summary>\u003cp class=\"history-note\">Verification checks the cited documents, not a complete history of the Nation. Last reviewed 2026-11-03.\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/eng/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Treaty No. 10 and Reports of Commissioners\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs Canada · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Treaty dates, signatories, commissioner, signing locations and the original bands’ participation. 1906 commissioner’s report and treaty signature blocks\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://www.canoelakefirstnation.com/about-us\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Our History\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Canoe Lake Cree First Nation · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Cree language and identity, traditional livelihoods, treaty leaders, Primrose Lake impacts and settlement. Our History, paragraphs 2–4\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/canoe_lake_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Canoe Lake First Nation\u003c/a>\u003cspan>University of Saskatchewan · Indigenous Saskatchewan Encyclopedia · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>1954 range impacts, the 1975 claim, acceptance in 1995 and June 1997 settlement. Christian Thompson entry, paragraphs 2–3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://otc.ca/about/historical-biographies/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Historical Biographies\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Office of the Treaty Commissioner · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Chief John Iron, Chief William Apisis (spelled Apsis here), McKenna’s full name and Treaty 10 negotiations. James Andrew Joseph McKenna and Treaty 10 biographies\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://skfn.ca/wp-content/uploads/2023/01/2018-09-25-FINAL-BirchCDP-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Birch Narrows Dene Nation Community Plan\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Birch Narrows Dene Nation · hosted by Saskatchewan First Nations · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Clear Lake/Peter Pond band history, 1972 Turnor Lake recognition and the 1990 Birch Narrows name change. Community History and timeline, printed pages 9–11\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://www.otc.ca/public/uploads/resource_photo/In_Their_Own_Land1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">In Their Own Land\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Office of the Treaty Commissioner · Further reading · not verified\u003c/span>\u003cp>Suggested further reading; not used to verify displayed claims. PDF could not be retrieved during review\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://www.rcaanc-cirnac.gc.ca/fra/1100100028874/1581292941464\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Treaty No. 10 · French transcription\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Crown-Indigenous Relations and Northern Affairs Canada · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Full spelling of Raphael Bedshi-Dekkge in the Clear Lake signature block. 1906 signature blocks\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://otc.ca/otc-at-the-treaty-10-gathering/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OTC at the Treaty 10 Gathering\u003c/a>\u003cspan>Office of the Treaty Commissioner · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Later account listing Canoe Lake as the signing location; differs from the contemporary report. Treaty signing locations paragraph\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ca href=\"https://teaching.usask.ca/indigenoussk/import/buffalo_river_dene_first_nation.php\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Buffalo River Dene First Nation\u003c/a>\u003cspan>University of Saskatchewan · Indigenous Saskatchewan Encyclopedia · Checked 2026-11-03\u003c/span>\u003cp>Peter Pond Band divided into Turnor Lake and Buffalo River in 1972. Historical overview\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/details>\u003c/section>"};
